            {
              "id": "art-body",
              "type": "column",
              "props": {
                "span": 9
              },
              "children": [
                {
                  "id": "lede",
                  "type": "text",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "If you operate a commercial truck, you've probably seen the warning lights that come on when diesel exhaust fluid levels get low. It's easy to think you can push it a little longer before refilling, especially during a busy workweek.",
                    "align": "left",
                    "width": "full",
                    "anchor": "post-lede"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "p-intro-1",
                  "type": "text",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "But running out of diesel exhaust fluid can create much bigger problems than most drivers expect. Modern diesel trucks rely heavily on emissions systems to operate properly. Ignoring low DEF warnings can lead to reduced engine performance, forced speed limitations, and expensive downtime that hurts your business. Understanding what happens when you run out of diesel exhaust fluid can help you avoid unnecessary repairs and keep your fleet moving efficiently.",
                    "align": "left",
                    "width": "full"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "h-what-is-def",
                  "type": "heading",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "What Is Diesel Exhaust Fluid?",
                    "headingLevel": 2,
                    "align": "left",
                    "anchor": "what-is-def"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "p-what-is-def-1",
                  "type": "text",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "Diesel exhaust fluid, commonly called DEF, is a solution made from purified water and urea. It works alongside your truck's Selective Catalytic Reduction (SCR) system to reduce harmful nitrogen oxide emissions produced by diesel engines. DEF is stored in a separate tank from diesel fuel and is automatically injected into the exhaust system while the truck is running.",
                    "align": "left",
                    "width": "full"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "p-what-is-def-2",
                  "type": "text",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "This process helps commercial trucks meet federal emissions regulations established by the Environmental Protection Agency (EPA). Without DEF, modern diesel trucks cannot properly control emissions, which is why manufacturers build in safeguards that limit vehicle performance when DEF levels get too low.",
                    "align": "left",
                    "width": "full"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "h-what-happens",
                  "type": "heading",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "What Happens When You Run Out of Diesel Exhaust Fluid?",
                    "headingLevel": 2,
                    "align": "left",
                    "anchor": "what-happens"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "p-what-happens-1",
                  "type": "text",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "The short answer is simple: your truck will eventually stop operating normally. What actually happens depends on the make, model, and system settings of your truck, but most commercial trucks follow a similar progression.",
                    "align": "left",
                    "width": "full"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "h3-what-happens-2",
                  "type": "heading",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "Warning Lights and Alerts",
                    "headingLevel": 3,
                    "align": "left",
                    "anchor": "warning-lights"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "p-what-happens-3",
                  "type": "text",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "Before the DEF tank runs completely empty, drivers usually receive multiple warnings through the dashboard system. These alerts often include:",
                    "align": "left",
                    "width": "full"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "list-what-happens-4",
                  "type": "prose-list",
                  "props": {
                    "ordered": false,
                    "items": [
                      {
                        "text": "Low DEF warning lights"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": "Countdown mileage notifications"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": "Reduced power warnings"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": "Emissions system alerts"
                      }
                    ]
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "p-what-happens-5",
                  "type": "text",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "Ignoring these warnings can quickly lead to operational restrictions.",
                    "align": "left",
                    "width": "full"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "h3-what-happens-6",
                  "type": "heading",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "Engine Derate Mode",
                    "headingLevel": 3,
                    "align": "left",
                    "anchor": "derate-mode"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "p-what-happens-7",
                  "type": "text",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "Once DEF levels become critically low or empty, most trucks enter what's called \"derate mode.\" In derate mode, the engine's power and speed are automatically reduced to encourage the driver to refill the DEF tank. Depending on the truck, you may notice:",
                    "align": "left",
                    "width": "full"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "list-what-happens-8",
                  "type": "prose-list",
                  "props": {
                    "ordered": false,
                    "items": [
                      {
                        "text": "Reduced acceleration"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": "Lower maximum speed"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": "Poor engine performance"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": "Limited RPMs"
                      }
                    ]
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "p-what-happens-9",
                  "type": "text",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "For fleet owners, this creates major operational challenges, especially during long hauls or tight delivery schedules.",
                    "align": "left",
                    "width": "full"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "h3-what-happens-10",
                  "type": "heading",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "The Truck May Eventually Fail to Restart",
                    "headingLevel": 3,
                    "align": "left",
                    "anchor": "wont-restart"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "p-what-happens-11",
                  "type": "text",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "One of the biggest misconceptions is that trucks simply keep running without DEF. In many cases, once the truck is shut off after DEF depletion, the engine may not restart until the system is refilled properly. That's where downtime becomes expensive.",
                    "align": "left",
                    "width": "full"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "h-why-never-ignore",
                  "type": "heading",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "Why DEF Problems Should Never Be Ignored",
                    "headingLevel": 2,
                    "align": "left",
                    "anchor": "why-never-ignore"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "p-why-never-ignore-1",
                  "type": "text",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "DEF issues are not always caused by an empty tank alone. Sometimes drivers experience problems because of:",
                    "align": "left",
                    "width": "full"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "checks-why-never-ignore-2",
                  "type": "check-list",
                  "props": {
                    "items": [
                      {
                        "text": "Contaminated DEF fluid"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": "Frozen DEF systems"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": "Sensor failures"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": "SCR system malfunctions"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": "DEF injector issues"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": "Crystallization buildup"
                      }
                    ]
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "p-why-never-ignore-3",
                  "type": "text",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "When these issues occur, professional diesel exhaust system repair is often necessary to prevent larger engine or emissions problems. Modern diesel emissions systems are complex, and trying to diagnose them without proper equipment can waste valuable time. That's why working with an experienced diesel mechanic for semi trucks matters.",
                    "align": "left",
                    "width": "full"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "co-why-never-ignore-4-row",
                  "type": "row",
                  "props": {
                    "gap": 0
                  },
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "id": "co-why-never-ignore-4",
                      "type": "column",
                      "props": {
                        "span": 12
                      },
                      "styles": {
                        "base": {
                          "borderWidth": 1,
                          "borderStyle": "solid",
                          "borderColor": "line",
                          "paddingTop": 26,
                          "paddingRight": 28,
                          "paddingBottom": 26,
                          "paddingLeft": 28,
                          "marginTop": 8,
                          "marginBottom": 34
                        }
                      },
                      "children": [
                        {
                          "id": "co-why-never-ignore-4-cta",
                          "type": "buttons",
                          "props": {
                            "align": "left",
                            "items": [
                              {
                                "ctaId": "schedule-service",
